<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 17</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 17 Walkthrough: Terrace</h1>

    <p>The Terrace is the seventeenth room of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and the first one in the chapter that is entirely outdoors. You get a paved dining corner under a black-and-white striped awning, a lounge deck with an L-shaped sofa and a green umbrella, a raised garden bed with purple flowers and one big rock, and two hanging pod chairs that nobody in this house ever sits in. There is no keypad on a wall and no door code to type at the exit. The level's two typed numbers live on a white crate and on a balance scale, and both of them are printed — you never have to guess a digit.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt at one frame per second. The crate code <b>7432</b> was read twice — once from the whole 3:12 frame and once from a 2.2x crop of the four digit cells — and both passes agreed. The scale display <b>7435</b> was read three times, at three crop scales, and all three agreed. Anything that only read one way, or that two passes read differently, is listed under "What this page could not verify" instead of being printed as an answer.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={17} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Terrace is room 17 of Chapter 3. The padded white crate's four-digit lock takes <b>7432</b>; the balance-scale panel above the two red pans reads <b>7435</b>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Open on the garden bed: the white crate with the yellow padlock is the first zoom, at <b>0:12</b>.</li>
        <li>Take the <b>Medallion</b> (0:24) — an olive-green disc with a three-arrow symbol.</li>
        <li>Work the two big boards: the <b>Balance</b> cushion scale (0:36) and the five-colour button panel on the dining wall (1:00).</li>
        <li>Count the three symbols — star, ring, chair — on the framed panel at <b>1:48</b>.</li>
        <li>Throw the wall switch at <b>2:33</b> to light the terrace, then collect the <b>crowbar</b> from the barrel at 2:48.</li>
        <li>Set the crate lock to <b>7432</b> at 3:12. The recording ends at 3:20 without a results card.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="KxjzFxup5ww" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 17 Terrace Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter 3"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 17 Terrace Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/KxjzFxup5ww/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 17 Terrace walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/KxjzFxup5ww/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The Terrace run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-hero-terrace.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 17 Terrace: an outdoor terrace with a dining table under a black-and-white striped awning, an L-shaped sofa with yellow cushions under a green umbrella, two hanging pod chairs, and a raised garden bed with purple flowers" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>One wide outdoor set, viewed from a high three-quarter angle, with the house wall along the back and left. The dining end is tiled, with a round table, white wire chairs and a hedge; the lounge end is a wooden deck with the sofa, a coffee table, two lanterns and a barrel tucked behind. Between them runs a raised bed of purple flowers with a single large rock sitting in it, and a small yellow toy mower parks on the deck for no reason the level ever explains.</p>
    <p>What makes the Terrace unusual is that it hides its puzzles in furniture rather than in locks. The two pans of a balance scale, a five-button panel on a wall, a framed counting puzzle and a box lid full of shallow golden wells are all things you would walk past in a real garden. The only two hard numbers in the room are the crate code and the number on the scale's panel, and neither is written anywhere else — which is exactly why so many players stall here.</p>
    <p>Structurally this is a hub, not a chain. The Medallion, the buttons, the counting panel and the scale can be worked in any order, and only the crate at the end actually gates anything. The wall switch at 2:33 is the one sequencing step the level insists on: throw it and the scene relights, the windows open, and the terrace starts sparkling.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Everything we could identify, and what it is for.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Padded white crate</td><td>Garden bed, front of the terrace</td><td>Yellow padlock on the hasp; four-digit lock takes <b>7432</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Medallion</td><td>First pickup, 0:24</td><td>Olive-green disc with a raised three-arrow symbol. Item card reads <b>Medallion</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Balance scale</td><td>Dining end, under the awning</td><td>Two red pans on a white frame; display panel reads <b>7435</b> with four red lights above it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Five-button panel</td><td>Dining wall</td><td>Round buttons in green, yellow, red, brown, red. No letters.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Striped wire chair</td><td>Dining table</td><td>Back lattice with five vertical stripes: red, orange, yellow, green, blue.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Counting panel</td><td>Framed close-up, 1:48</td><td><b>COUNT THE NUMBER FOR EACH SYMBOL</b>: star, ring, chair, with three dials.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>White box, brass lid</td><td>Close-up, 1:24</td><td>Five shallow golden wells in a row. Fits a medallion-shaped object.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wall switch</td><td>Beige tile wall by the French doors</td><td>Lever; lights the terrace and opens the windows at 2:42.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Barrel + crowbar</td><td>Behind the sofa, right of the deck</td><td>Weathered barrel with faded yellow and blue bands; <b>red crowbar</b> leaning on it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hanging pod chairs</td><td>Deck, left of the sofa</td><td>Two woven egg chairs with coloured cushions; sparkle once the lights are on.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Toy mower</td><td>Deck, near the garden bed</td><td>Small yellow push mower; scenery, not a puzzle.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The order below follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:00</span>
      <strong>Read the terrace before you touch it</strong>
      The level opens on a title card — <b>Level 17 / Terrace</b> — over a dimmed wide shot, then the camera settles and the HUD appears. Sweep it once: dining table and awning left, sofa and green umbrella right, garden bed with the big rock across the front, and the house wall with its blue French doors behind. Two hanging pod chairs sit between the dining end and the deck, and the barrel is behind the sofa where you will not see it until you pan.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-level-title.jpg" alt="The Level 17 Terrace title card in Rooms and Exits Chapter 3" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=0s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:12 · the first zoom</span>
      <strong>The white crate with the yellow padlock</strong>
      The very first thing the game zooms is a padded white wooden crate, worn at the edges, with a brass-yellow padlock hanging on its front hasp. No digits yet — this is the level introducing the object that will take the code at 3:00. Note where it lives: it sits low in the garden bed, partly screened by the purple flowers, which is why it is easy to lose again after the camera pulls back.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-garden-crate-padlock.jpg" alt="The padded white crate with a yellow padlock in the garden bed of the Terrace level" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=12s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:24 · the Medallion</span>
      <strong>An olive-green disc with a three-arrow symbol</strong>
      The first named item arrives as its own card: a thick olive-green disc, embossed with a three-arrow recycling-style symbol, and the word <b>Medallion</b> printed underneath. Take it. By 1:36 the inventory shows it sitting alongside a second, chair-shaped medallion, which matters because the white box with five golden wells at 1:24 is shaped to receive exactly this kind of disc.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-medallion.jpg" alt="The Medallion item card in the Terrace level, an olive-green disc with a three-arrow symbol" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=24s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:36 · the scale</span>
      <strong>“Balance” — and the panel that reads 7435</strong>
      Two red pans hang from a white frame, both loaded with stacked cushions: the left with a lime-green, a grey check and a brown patterned one, the right with orange, blue and yellow. Spare cushions wait on the right. The panel above them carries four red indicator lights and a white display that reads <b>7435</b>. Yes, the game spells the title <b>BLANACE</b>; that is in the build, not a typo here. The four red lights behave like progress markers, and the digits are the one number in the level that is not typed anywhere.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-balance-puzzle.jpg" alt="The Balance cushion balance puzzle in the Terrace level with two red pans, stacked pillows and a panel readiing 7435" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=36s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:36</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=42s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:42</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 0:54 · the chair</span>
      <strong>Five stripes on the back of a wire chair</strong>
      The dining chairs are white wire lattice, and one of them has five vertical colour stripes woven through the back: <b>red, orange, yellow, green, blue</b>, left to right. The camera holds on it for several seconds, which is the level's way of saying "this is a clue". It is the only striped object on the terrace, and five stripes is the same count as the five-button panel that comes six seconds later.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-dining-chair-stripes.jpg" alt="A white wire dining chair in the Terrace level with five vertical stripes in red, orange, yellow, green and blue" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=54s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:54</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:00 · the buttons</span>
      <strong>Five round buttons, no letters</strong>
      A brushed-metal panel with five round buttons, each ringed in silver. Left to right the colours are <b>green, yellow, red, brown, red</b> — the fourth is a dull brown-orange, the fifth is red again. Between 1:00 and 1:12 they are pressed in different orders and the pressed state is visible as a shadowed rim, but the recording never holds a solved panel long enough to read a final order off it. Treat the chair stripes as the intended hint and test orders that follow them.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-colour-buttons.jpg" alt="The five-button colour panel in the Terrace level with green, yellow, red, brown and red round buttons" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=60s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:00</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=72s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:24 · the box lid</span>
      <strong>Five golden wells in a brass insert</strong>
      A distressed white-painted box is pulled up as a full inspect view. Its lid holds a brass plate with <b>five round golden depressions</b> in a row — shallow wells, not buttons, not a digit display. The diameter matches the Medallion from 0:24, and five wells against one medallion in the inventory tells you the box is a receptacle puzzle rather than a code lock.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-white-box-lid.jpg" alt="The white wooden box in the Terrace level with a brass lid insert holding five round golden depressions" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=84s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:24</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:48 · the counting puzzle</span>
      <strong>“Count the number for each symbol”</strong>
      A framed panel with three yellow symbols in a row — a faceted star or gem, a plain ring, and a chair — and one number dial under each. The instruction reads <b>COUNT THE NUMBER FOR EACH SYMBOL</b>. The dials start at 0 0 0, and the recording starts turning them at 2:18, which is why you see 3 / 0 / 0 mid-solve. The symbols are objects in the room, not on the panel: count the star shapes, the rings and the chairs you can actually see.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-symbol-count-puzzle.jpg" alt="The count the number for each symbol puzzle in the Terrace level with a star, a ring and a chair above three number dials" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-counted-dials.jpg" alt="The three number dials of the symbol counting puzzle mid-solve in the Terrace level" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=108s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:48</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=138s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:18</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:33 · the switch</span>
      <strong>Throw the lever and the terrace relights</strong>
      A plain grey lever switch on beige tile, beside the blue French doors. Throw it and the whole scene changes at 2:42: the shutters and windows are open, the terrace is brighter, and both hanging pod chairs throw sparkles. This is the level's one hard sequencing step — the barrel, the crate and the last puzzles are all easier to see after it, so do not save it for last.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-wall-switch.jpg" alt="The grey lever wall switch beside the blue French doors in the Terrace level" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=153s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:33</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:48 · the barrel</span>
      <strong>A red crowbar leaning on a painted barrel</strong>
      Behind the sofa stands a weathered wooden barrel, banded in faded yellow and blue-grey paint, and a <b>red crowbar</b> is leaning against it. The camera zooms the crowbar, not the barrel, so the crowbar is the pickup. It is the only bright red object in that corner of the deck, which makes it easy to spot again if you back out too fast.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-barrel-crowbar.jpg" alt="A weathered barrel in the Terrace level with a red crowbar leaning against it" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=168s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:48</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 3:00 to 3:12 · the code</span>
      <strong>Set the crate lock to 7432</strong>
      The crate comes back as a close-up, the yellow padlock is opened, and the lock face appears: four dark digit cells over a red button on the left and a green button on the right. The player scrolls through 6 0 0 0 at 3:06 and settles on <b>7 4 3 2</b> at 3:12. Read it off the frames yourself: 7, 4, 3, 2, four cells, no decimal point. Green confirms, red clears.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-crate-lock-7432.jpg" alt="The four-digit lock on the white Terrace crate set to 7432 with red and green buttons underneath" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=186s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:06</a> · <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KxjzFxup5ww&t=192s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3:12</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 3:18 · where the video stops</span>
      <strong>Sparkles, then the file ends</strong>
      The last usable frames pull back to the terrace with the windows open and sparkles running along the pod chairs and the hedge. There is no crate-open animation, no results card and no reward in the file, which runs out at 3:20. If you are following along, 3:18 is simply where this recording stops, not where the level does.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l17-terrace-night.jpg" alt="The Terrace level at the end of the recording with the windows open and sparkles around the hanging pod chairs" width="1200" height="676" loading="lazy" />
