    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>You type 7435 into the crate.</b> That is the most common wrong turn in this level, because 7435 is the number you spend the longest looking at — it is printed on the scale's panel and it looks like an answer. It is not the crate code. The crate takes <b>7432</b>. If the green button does nothing, check the last digit first.</p>
    <p><b>You never find the second half of the terrace.</b> The barrel with the crowbar is behind the sofa, and the crate sits low in the flower bed. Both are off the first screen you see. Pan right for the barrel, pan down for the crate — the recording does both and it looks like the camera is just idling.</p>
    <p><b>The scale's pans never balance.</b> Four red indicator lights sit above the 7435 display and they light up as progress markers, not as a pass/fail gate. If you are moving cushions and the lights are climbing, you are doing the right thing; the pans are not supposed to sit level until the puzzle is done.</p>
    <p><b>You read the five buttons as a colour code with letters.</b> There are no letters on that panel. Five round buttons, five colours, no printed order. The dining chair's five stripes are the only ordered colour clue in the level, which is why the two objects are six seconds apart in the recording.</p>
    <p><b>You count butterflies.</b> Nothing in the Terrace asks you to count objects in general — the counting panel names three specific symbols. If you are counting flowers or cushions, you are one step off; find the star, the ring and the chair.</p>

    <h2>Where the Terrace sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and its rooms run through the house and out into the grounds: Dining Room, Living Room, Study Room, Guest Toilet, Workshop, Guest Bedroom, Master Bathroom, Kids Playroom, Backyard, Garden, Kitchen, Driveway, Kids Bedroom, Storage Room, Garage, Attic, <b>Terrace</b>, Basement, Garden Shed, Kids Bedroom 2. The Terrace is room <b>17</b>, straight after the Attic and immediately before the Basement.</p>
    <p>Mechanically it is a holiday from the Attic's enclosed clutter. The Attic was boxes and rafters and one long chain; the Terrace is open air, bright, and almost entirely parallel puzzles. The only dependency is the wall switch — everything else can be picked up or attempted in whatever order you notice it, which makes the Terrace one of the friendlier rooms in the back half of the chapter.</p>

